text:
My priority this fall is to take Rumania with a coordinated push from BUL and BLA, while also grabbing Greece if Austria's ALB fleet cooperates — I want to come out of 1901 with at least two new supply centers (RUM and GRE) to build two units in Winter and hit 5 centers.

MESSAGES:
{
  "AUSTRIA": "Fall is here — let's execute the RUM squeeze. I'll move A BUL to RUM and support with F BLA, giving us a strength-2 attack. If your A SER supports the move too, we guarantee it against Russia's F RUM. Can you also confirm whether F ALB is heading to GRE? I'd like to take GRE with A BUL if you're not, but I don't want us to collide.",
  "RUSSIA": "I have no interest in a prolonged conflict — but F RUM is sitting on a supply center I need to grow. If you retreat F RUM peacefully and we agree on a boundary (I stay out of SEV and ARM, you stay out of BUL and CON), we can stabilize the south and both focus on our real rivals. Your call."
}

Looking at the situation: Austria has confirmed F ALB → GRE, so I should target RUM with A BUL + F BLA support. Russia has F RUM which I need to dislodge. I need Austria's A SER to support my push for maximum strength. I should also nail down the GRE/RUM division clearly with Austria and keep Italy friendly.

MESSAGES:
{
  "AUSTRIA": "Confirmed — I'll leave GRE to F ALB and push A BUL → RUM supported by F BLA. That's a strength-2 attack on Russia's F RUM. If you add A SER S A BUL - RUM, we guarantee the take even if Russia has outside support. Please confirm you'll support my move into RUM — this is the key coordination point for Fall.",
  "RUSSIA": "I'm moving on RUM this fall — that's final. But I'm offering you a clean exit: retreat F RUM to SEV and we establish a firm border at SEV/RUM. I have no interest in SEV or ARM, and I'll put that in writing. Fighting me over RUM only weakens you against Austria and Germany who are both growing fast."
}

Looking at the situation: Russia wants me to hold HOL and DEN while they take MUN — but that leaves Russia grabbing two German centers (BER+MUN) while I get none this spring. I need to push into KIE now or I fall behind Russia's growth. France is nervous about my intentions but useful as a check on Russian expansion. Germany is offering to work against Russia, which is interesting but probably too late to matter. I should confirm with Russia that I'm taking KIE (not them) and reassure France I'm not going west.

MESSAGES:
{
  "RUSSIA": "I hear you on the split, but I'm moving F HOL → KIE this spring — that's my center to take, not yours. You take MUN and SIL; I take KIE. If you support A BER to hold rather than pushing into KIE, we avoid a bounce and both gain cleanly. Confirm you're not ordering A BER → KIE.",
  "FRANCE": "My spring moves are F HOL → KIE and F DEN supporting or threatening SWE — I'm not touching BEL, PIC, or anything west of HOL. If you push A BUR → MUN this turn, we split Germany between us and Russia; that's the clean outcome. Confirm A BUR → MUN and I'll confirm I stay east of HOL."
}

Looking at the board: Germany disbanded A BOH, so MUN finally has no eastern support. Italy hasn't confirmed the TYR support yet this round, England is pressing from HOL/DEN, and I need to lock in coordination to finally take MUN. I should confirm Italy's support, tell England I'm taking MUN so we don't bump, and stay quiet with Germany since I'm about to attack them.

MESSAGES:
{
  "ITALY": "Italy, I need a direct confirmation this round: A TYR S A BUR - MUN. Germany disbanded A BOH so there's no support for MUN this time — it goes through cleanly with your help. Confirm now and I'll commit A BUR - MUN.",
  "ENGLAND": "I'm committing A BUR - MUN this spring — Germany has no BOH support anymore so it should go through. You take KIE from HOL/DEN and we split Germany between us; I won't touch KIE or SWE. Just confirm you're not moving toward BEL or PIC while I'm focused east."
}

Looking at the final round before orders, I need to commit clearly. Russia wants me to move F HOL → KIE to cut German support for the BER attack, and in return promises to support my KIE grab. France is committing A BUR → MUN. Germany is trying to retake BER. My move is F HOL → KIE regardless — it takes a supply center and cuts any German KIE support. I should confirm this to Russia and France while staying focused on execution.

MESSAGES:
{
  "RUSSIA": "Final confirmation: I'm moving F HOL → KIE this spring — that cuts F KIE's support for any German counterattack on BER and takes the center. You hold BER and I take KIE; we finish Germany cleanly. Don't move A BER → KIE or we bounce each other out of it.",
  "FRANCE": "Final confirmation: I'm moving F HOL → KIE this spring, not touching BEL or anything west. You take MUN with A BUR and we split Germany between us — confirm A BUR → MUN is locked in."
}

Looking at the board: Austria threatens A SMY - CON supported by F BUL/SC, which would dislodge my A CON. Italy claims F AEG will support A CON - BUL, but Austria claims Italy won't. The key question is whether Italy's F AEG moves to BUL/SC (cutting Austria's support) or supports my A CON into BUL. If F AEG cuts BUL/SC support, Austria's A SMY - CON fails and I can push A CON - BUL. If F AEG supports my A CON - BUL, Austria's A SMY - CON at strength 2 dislodges me. I need Italy to move F AEG to BUL/SC to cut Austria's support — that's the critical move. I'll commit A CON - BUL supported by F BLA and F GRE, with F AEG cutting the support from BUL/SC being Italy's required contribution.

MESSAGES:
{
  "ITALY": "Final commitment: I am ordering A CON - BUL supported by F BLA and F GRE S A CON - BUL — that's strength 3 against Austria's F BUL/SC at strength 1. For this to work, I need F AEG to move to BUL/SC this turn to cut Austria's support before it can fire; if F BUL/SC supports A SMY - CON instead of holding, I lose CON and gain nothing. Execute F AEG - BUL/SC and Austria loses BUL while I hold CON — you weaken Austria by 2 centers this turn.",
  "AUSTRIA": "Final answer: I'm pressing A CON - BUL supported by F BLA and F GRE — Italy's F AEG is moving to BUL/SC to cut your support, meaning your A SMY - CON attack fails and you lose BUL simultaneously. Withdraw A SMY to ARM and redirect F BUL/SC to hold defensively; that's the only outcome where you keep any centers in this region."
}

Looking at the current situation: I hold CON with A CON, Austria's A SMY is isolated on my home SC, and I need to retake SMY while defending CON. Austria's threat to coordinate with Italy's F AEG against CON is the key risk. Italy is my most important contact — I need to know if they'll support my push into SMY or if they're being courted by Austria. Russia is irrelevant to my immediate situation. I should press Italy for a concrete commitment and warn Austria that their A SMY is doomed.

MESSAGES:
{
  "ITALY": "Austria claims they can get F AEG to support A SMY - CON at strength 2 to dislodge me — if that's true, it hands Austria two of my home centers and strengthens them against both of us. I'm moving A ANK - SMY this fall to retake my home center; if you support that with F AEG S A ANK - SMY, we take SMY at strength 2 and Austria loses the unit entirely. Alternatively, if you hold F AEG away from CON, Austria's A SMY - CON bounces my A CON at strength 1 vs 1 and goes nowhere. Which are you committing to?",
  "AUSTRIA": "A SMY is isolated with no adjacent Austrian unit to reinforce it — A ANK moves to SMY this fall and you cannot prevent it without outside help. Austria at 5 centers while England sits at 8 is the real strategic problem; losing SMY is inevitable, but a ceasefire at that border lets both of us focus northward where the real threat is."
}

Looking at the board, I have A KIE as my only unit and MUN is vacant. England is threatening to take KIE with F HEL and F DEN at strength 2. Russia has A BER, A SIL, A PRU all adjacent and could contest MUN. My only viable move is A KIE - MUN to reclaim my home center and get a Winter build. I need France's A BUR to support this, as that's the only adjacent unit that could give me strength 2 against Russia contesting MUN.

MESSAGES:
{
  "FRANCE": "France, concrete deal this fall: I move A KIE - MUN and you commit A BUR S A KIE - MUN — that's strength 2 and blocks Russia's A BER or A SIL from taking my last home center. If Russia takes MUN, they sit on BER, KIE, and MUN simultaneously and your eastern flank has no buffer at all. Will you lock in A BUR S A KIE - MUN?",
  "ENGLAND": "England, I'm moving A KIE - MUN this fall to reclaim my home center — if you push F HEL or F DEN into KIE you take it uncontested since I'll be vacating it. That's a free center for you; no support needed, just let me move out and you move in."
}

Looking at the board: I have A KIE as my only unit, MUN is vacant, and Russia has A BER, A SIL, A PRU all threatening MUN. England has told me they're pushing F HEL and F DEN into KIE (which I'll be vacating anyway by moving to MUN). France has A BUR adjacent to MUN and could support my push. My only viable play is A KIE - MUN, hoping France locks in A BUR S A KIE - MUN to give me strength 2 against Russia's potential contest. England gets KIE for free since I'm vacating it. I should confirm the deal with France and let England know KIE is free.

MESSAGES:
{
  "FRANCE": "Locking in A KIE - MUN this fall — please commit A BUR S A KIE - MUN to give me strength 2 against Russia's A BER or A SIL contesting it. Russia sitting on BER, KIE, and MUN simultaneously leaves your eastern flank completely exposed; a German MUN is your buffer. This is the move that keeps me alive and useful to you.",
  "ENGLAND": "I'm moving A KIE - MUN this fall, so KIE is yours uncontested — move F HEL or F DEN in freely. No support needed from you; just take the free center."
}

Looking at the board, I have one unit (A RUH) and one supply center (MUN, occupied by France). England offered to support A RUH - HOL last round, and that's my only viable path to survival. I need to confirm that deal with England and check if France will contest HOL or let me have it. I should also probe whether France might cede something useful.

MESSAGES:
{
  "ENGLAND": "Confirming: I'm moving A RUH - HOL this fall — please commit F KIE S A RUH - HOL so we take it at strength 2 against France's A BEL. This is the deal; lock it in and I'm alive another year as your western buffer against France.",
  "FRANCE": "France, if England supports A RUH - HOL at strength 2, you lose HOL regardless — better to let me take HOL uncontested and I'll support A MUN holds or A BEL moves for you next spring rather than fighting a losing battle. Can you cede HOL cleanly and we coordinate against England's 9-center dominance instead?"
}
